speechtext:
I am not all wrong. The government does not even know what it is asking parliament to do. What the act means is that if parliament by order in council cancels any of these provisions, they cannot be revived. That is exactly what the government should put before this house to deal with. If they simply want this act to retain the existence of the board they should cancel all of the wartime regulations except the one which provides for the board itself. When they do that, then under the act that was passed and to which the hon. member has referred it cannot be revived. But this is an order still continuing; this is an order still in effect.
That is exactly what the government should have done-cancelled by order in council all the provisions except that one part of this particular wartime order that is now carried forward and which creates the board necessary to administer the rent control provisions of the other order. I should like to point out some of the other provisions that are still here so that hon. members will know what is being dealt with. As an illustration, section 4, subsection (1) (h), provides:
(1) The board shall have power
(h)	to require any person to obtain licences or permits from the board or from any person specified by the board; to prescribe the terms and conditions under which and the manner and circumstances in which such licences or permits or amendment thereof shall be obtained;
Transitional Measures Act
Then it goes on to point out what those licences may be for, and the next paragraph follows:
(i)	to amend, suspend or cancel any licence or permit issued or granted;
(j)	to prescribe the quantities of, the manner in which, and the terms and conditions under which, any goods or services may be bought, sold, supplied, distributed, delivered or used within prescribed periods of time and to prohibit purchase, sale, supply, distribution, delivery or use except in accordance with such prescription;
(k)	to require any person owning or having possession, control or power to dispose of any goods or services-
May I underline the word "services":
-to deal with, use, dispose of or supply any such goods or services, in such manner as may be prescribed by the board; and no use, disposition or supply of any such goods or services in accordance with such manner shall constitute infringement of any mark, design, or other private or proprietary right;
Then it goes on to deal with manufacturing provisions. Those are the powers that are being continued. Some hon. members may be under the impression that an undertaking was given that those powers would not be exercised in the statement made by the Minister of Finance on March 24, but that is not what the Minister of Finance said. The Minister of Finance pointed out what powers were still in existence, which were the six orders to which the hon. member for Eglinton (Mr. Fleming) has referred, which are as follows: 8528, 9029, 34/4433, 3122, 4600 and 6233. The two important orders are 8528, the one covering the wartime prices and trade regulations, and 9029 covering the wartime leasehold regulations.

The purpose of these Papers is twofold:
(1)	to make available in one place and in convenient form, as a supplement to the Budget, some of the more comprehensive indicators of economic conditions prepared by the Dominion Bureau of Statistics and other Government agencies, together with brief comments;
(2)	to present a preliminary review of the Government accounts for the fiscal year ending on March 31, 1950.
